Smart Banking System

A full-stack banking system supporting concurrent transactions and transaction rollback. The backend is written in C++17 and provides a RESTful API; the frontend uses React to build interactive dashboards.

GitHub: https://github.com/yaoyangzhang1234894/mini-bank-system

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Technology Stack

Backend: C++17, httplib, nlohmann/json

Database: SQLite3

Concurrency Control: std::mutex, std::atomic, std::thread

Frontend: React, Vite, Tailwind CSS, Axios
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Core Functions

Account Management: Create account, query account information, close account

Deposit/Withdrawal: Supports balance verification, negative balance blocking, and account status checking

Transfer: Supports transaction rollback; automatically restores the source account balance when a transfer fails

Concurrency Control: Uses mutex to protect shared resources and atomic to count the number of transactions

Transaction Log: Records the type, amount, and status of each transaction

REST API: All functions are exposed via HTTP API; the frontend can call them across platforms.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Technical Highlights

Concurrency Security: Uses ‘std::mutex’ to protect account balances; ‘std::atomic’ counts total transactions to avoid race conditions.

Transaction rollback: If a transfer fails to reach the target account, the source account balance is automatically restored to ensure data consistency.

RAII and move semantics: ‘std::unique_ptr’ manages mutexes; the ‘Account’ class implements move construction and move assignment.

Layered architecture: ‘Bank’ → ’Account‘ → ‘mydbms’ → SQLite, clear responsibilities, easy to extend.

Front-end and back-end separation: The back-end provides a REST API, and the front-end calls it via Axios, resulting in a clear and maintainable structure.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Project Structure

Back-end (C++)

- Account.h / Account.cpp — Account class, manages balance, username, and password; built-in mutex ensures concurrency safety.

- Bank.h / Bank.cpp — Main bank logic, handles account management, deposits, withdrawals, and transfers; supports concurrent transaction simulation; triggers rollback when a transfer fails.

- Transaction.h / Transaction.cpp — Transaction class, records transactions. ID, Type, Amount, Timestamp, and Status

- mydbms.h / mydbms.cpp — SQLite wrapper layer, responsible for database and table creation, CRUD operations

- server.cpp — HTTP server, based on httplib, providing REST API endpoints

- main.cpp — Command-line entry point, demonstrating all core functions

Frontend (React)

- react/bank-frontend/ — The entire frontend project

- src/App.jsx — Main interface, containing all page logic

- src/api.js — Wraps Axios, responsible for communication with the backend

- src/App.css — Styles


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
How to Run

Environment Requirements

- C++ compiler (g++ 9+ or clang++ 10+)

- Node.js 18+

- SQLite3

Backend

cd v20260924
g++ -std=c++17 -pthread. The backend runs at http://localhost:8080.

The frontend is run as follows:

cd v20260924/react/bank-frontend
npm install
npm run dev

The frontend runs at http://localhost:5173.

Open your browser and access http://localhost:5173 to see the bank system interface.
